SECTION 3: VALIDATION PLAN
================================================================================

3.1 PHASE 1: AGENT-BASED MODELING (6-8 months)

OBJECTIVE
Simulate regional economies under ROBUC to forecast:
  - Cost trajectories (% GDP spent on care)
  - Outcome improvements (life expectancy, chronic disease onset, mortality)
  - Equity metrics (disparity closure, boutique market share)
  - Provider stability (bid competition, market concentration, churn)

MODEL SCOPE
  - Population representation: 50-100 synthetic regions (20K each).
  - Agent types: Advocates (boutique/incumbent), patients, Auction House 
    operators, government.
  - Parameter sweep: Bonus pool size, metric stringency, utility/cost curves, 
    boutique startup cost, telemedicine adoption rate.
  - Baseline: US current status quo cost/outcomes; calibrate to recent bundled-
    payment empirics (BPCI-A, CJR, TEAM).

KEY OUTPUTS
  1. Cost Path: Forecast national medical spending (as % GDP) over 10 years 
     under ROBUC. Provide uncertainty bands (±2 SD).
  2. Outcome Path: Disability-Free Life Expectancy, time-to-first-chronic-
     disease, mortality, and disparity metrics vs. baseline.
  3. Boutique Survival: Market share of boutique Advocates by region type 
     (rural/urban/mixed).
  4. Churn/Stability: Advocate turnover, bonus variance, regional re-bid 
     frequency.
  5. Sensitivity Analysis: 3D visualization (Health vs. Cost vs. Equity) under 
     ±20% variance in key assumptions.

3.2 PHASE 2: INTERNATIONAL BENCHMARKING (4-6 months, concurrent with ABM)

OBJECTIVE
Establish empirical anchors for:
  - Cost convergence from US levels toward OECD peers.
  - Outcome improvements achievable through bundled/capitated payment + outcome 
    incentives.
  - Equity gains from transparent, competitive provider markets.

COMPARABLE SYSTEMS
  - Kaiser Permanente (US): Integrated delivery, capitated payment, prevention-
    focused. Reported 6-8 years longer life expectancy, 50-70% higher Stage 1 
    cancer diagnoses.
  - Netherlands (ACOs/bundled): Regional primary care networks, outcome 
    incentives. ~15-20% utilization reduction, modest cost savings.
  - Germany (Bismarck/bundled): Competitive sickness funds, episode-based 
    bundling (Gesundes Kinzigtal). 10-25% cost reductions in select conditions.
  - Australia (Managed Care): Episode-based bundling for orthopedics. 20-30% 
    cost savings post-acute care.
  - OECD Peers: Canada, UK, France, Nordic countries spend 8-11% GDP on 
    universal care with outcomes comparable to or better than US.

KEY QUESTIONS
  1. How quickly does US move from 17.2% GDP (2024) to 8-10% under competition 
     + outcome incentives?
  2. What is the ceiling on utilization reduction? (Bundled payment evidence: 
     10-25% for low-value services; 0-5% for high-value.)
  3. Are equity gains (disparity closure) sustained under market pressure? 
     (Limited evidence; hypothesize SBA boutique support helps.)

DELIVERABLE
A benchmarking synthesis comparing ROBUC projections (from ABM) to empirical 
precedents, with confidence intervals for cost/outcome/equity assumptions.

3.3 PHASE 3: CONTROL REGION FEASIBILITY STUDY (3-4 months, optional)

If academic partners identify a willing state/city, design a SMALL FFS (Fee-
for-Service) control region (~20K people, uninsured/Medicaid majority) to:
  - Validate baseline health/cost data collection infrastructure.
  - Demonstrate Auction House data platform functionality.
  - Pilot patient record sandboxing and researcher blinding.
  - Confirm metric calculation and reporting timelines.

NOTE: This is a feasibility test, not a preliminary randomized trial. Expect 
6-8 months.

