SECTION 8: ECONOMIC LOGIC & BENCHMARKS
================================================================================

8.1 DEMAND CURVE SHIFT

Prevention-focused outcome incentives are expected to shift healthcare demand 
leftward (reduce low-value utilization):

  - Low-value services (e.g., unnecessary imaging, overtreatment): 40-60% 
    utilization reduction.
  - Moderate-value services (e.g., some elective procedures): 10-20% reduction.
  - High-value services (emergency care, critical diagnostics): 0-5% reduction.

Underlying mechanism: Advocates have financial incentive to prevent disease onset 
and avoid costly complications, shifting patient population toward prevention, 
early diagnosis, and chronic disease management.

8.2 INTERNATIONAL PRECEDENTS

KAISER PERMANENTE (US INTEGRATED DELIVERY MODEL)
  - Structure: Capitated, integrated delivery with prevention incentives.
  - Outcomes: 6-8 years longer life expectancy vs. FFS baseline.
  - Cancer Detection: 50-70% higher Stage 1 cancer diagnoses (early intervention).
  - Cost: ~10-15% lower per-capita than US FFS average.

MANAGED CARE MODELS (Netherlands, Germany, Australia)
  - Episode-bundled payment for acute/surgical conditions.
  - Reported utilization reduction: 10-25% for low-value services.
  - Modest cost savings: 5-15% vs. FFS baseline.

OECD PEERS (Canada, UK, France, Nordic Countries)
  - Universal coverage with capitated/bundled payment structures.
  - Spending: 8-11% GDP (vs. US 17.2% in 2024).
  - Health outcomes: Comparable or superior life expectancy, preventable 
    mortality, maternal/infant health.

8.3 COST-EFFECTIVENESS THRESHOLDS

Standard cost-effectiveness thresholds guide intervention selection:
  - UK (NICE): £20,000-30,000 per QALY gained.
  - Australia (PBAC): A$50,000 per QALY gained.
  - US (informal): $100,000-150,000 per QALY gained.

Under ROBUC bonus metrics, Advocates will naturally prioritize interventions 
exceeding these thresholds, aligning with global best practice.

8.4 BUNDLED PAYMENT EVIDENCE (BPCI-A, CJR, TEAM)

Recent bundled payment demonstrations (US):
  - 56-60% of episodes show cost savings + quality gains.
  - Cost reduction: 5-15% per episode (with quality maintained).
  - Quality improvement: 10-25% reduction in readmissions, infections, 
    complications.

ROBUC mechanism differs: Rather than bundling individual episodes, ROBUC 
capitate entire regional population with outcome incentives, creating stronger 
prevention signal.

8.5 INTERNATIONAL CONVERGENCE HYPOTHESIS

Underlying thesis:

Price-competitive care delivery + outcome incentives will lower US medical 
spending toward international norms (8-11% GDP). Offsetting factor: US leads 
global drug R&D; international sales subsidize innovation. Under ROBUC, US drug 
pricing remains market-driven (Advocates negotiate independently), preserving 
R&D incentives while reducing administrative waste and low-value utilization.

Projected convergence timeline: 8-12 years post-implementation.

8.6 EXPECTED BIDDING BEHAVIOR

Smart Advocates will pursue region-specific strategies:

AFFLUENT REGIONS
  - Lower fixed rates, higher expected bonuses (confident in outcome targets).
  - Attract high-performing clinicians with bonus upside.

UNDERSERVED REGIONS
  - Higher fixed rates (higher baseline need), lower bonuses.
  - Build permanent infrastructure: primary care clinics, telehealth hubs, 
    community health worker networks, address food deserts, recreation, 
    mental health.
  - Partner with SBA boutique programs to reduce operational risk.
  - Negotiate bulk drug/device pricing to achieve cost targets.

MIXED REGIONS
  - Differentiated bidding by subpopulation health profile.
  - Leverage Auction House data to identify high-utilization cohorts and 
    design targeted interventions.

Result: Market-driven capital allocation to underserved areas, without explicit 
subsidies. Advocates search the market for cost-competitive substitute goods and
services. 

