SECTION 10: DATA ARCHITECTURE
================================================================================

10.1 CENTRALIZED VS. DISTRIBUTED RECORDS

PATIENT RECORDS ARCHITECTURE
  - Advocates maintain primary clinical records (independent EHRs).
  - Auction Houses maintain a CENTRALIZED DATA WAREHOUSE:
    * Claims data (insurance, pharmacy, laboratory).
    * Aggregated clinical summaries (diagnoses, procedures, medications).
    * Outcome metrics (mortality, hospitalizations, employment status).
    * Patient demographics and insurance status.
  - Patient access: Sandboxed via Auction House portal (own record only, 
    no provider identification).

RESEARCHER ACCESS
  - Blinded data: Researchers access de-identified, aggregated patient cohorts 
    via Auction House.
  - No Advocate/payer identification in research datasets.
  - Researchers pay Auction House fee ($1K-100K per project, tiered by cohort 
    size).

DOCTOR AUTONOMY
  - Doctors retain full control of clinical records and EHR systems.
  - No forced interoperability or record migration.
  - Information sharing: Doctors submit claims + summary data to Auction House 
    for metric calculation (via standard data exchange protocols).

10.2 SECURITY & PRIVACY

DATA MINIMIZATION
  - Only data necessary for metric calculation is shared with Auction Houses.
  - Researchers never access raw clinical notes or identifiable information.

ENCRYPTION & ACCESS CONTROL
  - All data in transit: TLS 1.3 or equivalent.
  - All data at rest: AES-256 encryption.
  - Audit logging: All data access tracked and reviewed quarterly.

PATIENT CONSENT & OPT-OUT
  - Patients consent to data sharing for outcome measurement (mandatory).
  - Patients can opt out of research data sales (but not outcome metric 
    calculation).
  - Annual consent renewal (re-affirm or withdraw).

